## Why This Is Asked
- Assesses ability to build robust, single-line data pipelines over multi-file logs, including compressed files, without external dependencies. 
- Tests time-window handling, cross-service aggregation, and per-tenant bucketing under rotation. 

## Key Concepts
- Stream processing with standard UNIX tools (find, xargs, zcat, awk). 
- JSON field extraction via regex in awk; resilient to line variations. 
- Time-window filtering using START/END derived from date; cross-file aggregation. 
- Sorting by computed metric (error rate) without extra tools.

## Code Example
```javascript
START=$(date -u -d "-6 hours" +"%Y-%m-%dT%H")
END=$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H")
find /var/log/cluster -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) -print0 | \
  xargs -0 -I{} sh -lc 'case "{}" in *.gz) zcat "{}" ;; *) cat "{}" ;; esac' | \
  awk -v s="$START" -v e="$END" '{
    if (match($0, /"ts":"([^"]+)"/, a)) ts=a[1]; hour=substr(ts,1,13); gsub(/T/," ",hour); hour_key=hour;
    if (match($0, /"tenant":"([^"]+)"/, b)) tenant=b[1];
    if (match($0, /"lvl":"([^"]+)"/, c)) lvl=c[1];
    if (hour_key && tenant && hour_key>=s && hour_key<=e) {
      total[tenant] += 1; if (lvl=="ERROR") errors[tenant] += 1;
    }
  }
  END { for (t in total) { rate = total[t] ? errors[t] / total[t] : 0; printf "%s\t%.6f\n", t, rate } }
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to include per-hour breakdowns in addition to the overall top 3? 
- How would you handle non-UTC timestamps or malformed JSON lines?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical Unix log processing skills including mixed format parsing, gzip archive handling, path-derived service context, and time-window filtering logic.

## Key Concepts
- Robust streaming parsing of two log formats (ISO timestamp and JSON)
- Time window filtering with epoch conversion
- Per-service aggregation and top-N result extraction
- Graceful handling of unreadable files and gzip archives

## Code Example
```bash
find /logs -name 'service-*/[0-9]*.log*' -type f 2>/dev/null | \
while IFS= read -r f; do \
  if [[ $f == *.gz ]]; then \
    zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue; \
  else \
    cat "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue; \
  fi; \
done | awk '
BEGIN { 
  "date +%s" | getline now; 
  cutoff = now - 3600;
}
{
  # Parse ISO timestamp format
  if (match($0, /^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/)) {
    ts = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH);
    cmd = "date -d \"" ts "\" +%s";
    cmd | getline epoch;
    close(cmd);
    if (epoch >= cutoff && $2 == "ERROR:") {
      service = FILENAME;
      gsub(/.*\//, "", service);
      gsub(//.*/, "", service);
      msg = substr($0, index($0, "ERROR:") + 6);
      count[service "|" msg]++;
    }
  }
  # Parse JSON format
  else if ($0 ~ /^{/) {
    # Extract ts, lvl, msg from JSON
    if (match($0, /"ts"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"[^"]*"/)) {
      json_ts = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH);
      gsub(/.*"/, "", json_ts);
      gsub(/".*/, "", json_ts);
      cmd = "date -d \"" json_ts "\" +%s";
      cmd | getline epoch;
      close(cmd);
      if (epoch >= cutoff) {
        if (match($0, /"lvl"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"ERROR"/)) {
          if (match($0, /"msg"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"[^"]*"/)) {
            json_msg = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH);
            gsub(/.*"/, "", json_msg);
            gsub(/".*/, "", json_msg);
            service = FILENAME;
            gsub(/.*\//, "", service);
            gsub(//.*/, "", service);
            count[service "|" json_msg]++;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
END {
  # Aggregate counts by service and output top 5 per service
  for (key in count) {
    split(key, parts, "|");
    service = parts[1];
    msg = parts[2];
    service_count[service "|" msg] = count[key];
    services[service] = 1;
  }

  for (svc in services) {
    print "\n=== " svc " ===";
    n = 0;
    for (key in service_count) {
      split(key, parts, "|");
      if (parts[1] == svc) {
        messages[++n] = parts[2];
        values[n] = service_count[key];
      }
    }

    # Simple bubble sort for top 5
    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (j = i + 1; j <= n; j++) {
        if (values[j] > values[i]) {
          temp = values[i]; values[i] = values[j]; values[j] = temp;
          temp = messages[i]; messages[i] = messages[j]; messages[j] = temp;
        }
      }
    }

    for (i = 1; i <= (n < 5 ? n : 5); i++) {
      printf "%d: %s\n", values[i], messages[i];
    }
  }
}'
```

This solution handles mixed log formats, gzip archives, time-based filtering, per-service aggregation, and gracefully skips unreadable files.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to parse mixed log formats, handle multi-line events, and perform cross-file aggregation under log rotation. It assesses proficiency with standard UNIX pipeline tools and robust error handling.

## Key Concepts
- Parsing mixed plain-text and JSON log formats
- Time-window filtering with dynamic cutoff calculation
- Handling multi-line trace blocks and TRACE context markers
- Streaming large log sets efficiently with find and while loops
- Gracefully skipping unreadable files via 2>/dev/null redirects

## Code Example
```bash
# Stream all .log and .log.gz files, skip unreadable
find /logs/trace/ -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) 2>/dev/null | \
while read -r f; do 
  case "$f" in 
    *.gz) zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null;; 
    *) cat "$f" 2>/dev/null;; 
  esac
done | awk -v cutoff=$(date -d '2 hours ago' '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M') '
  # Parse JSON lines with ERROR level, extract trace_id
  /^\{/ { 
    if ($0 ~ /"lvl":.*"ERROR"/) { 
      match($0, /"trace_id":"([^"]+)"/, t); 
      if (t[1] && $0 >= cutoff) errcnt[t[1]]++ 
    } 
  }
  # Parse plain-text lines with ERROR level
  /^\d{4}-/ { 
    if ($0 ~ /ERROR/) { 
      match($0, /\[([^\]]+)\]/, t); 
      if (t[1]) errcnt[t[1]]++ 
    } 
  }
  END { for (id in errcnt) print id ": " errcnt[id] }
'
```

## Follow-up Questions
- **Missing timestamps or time zones:** Use explicit timezone handling with TZ environment variable, implement default-fallback logic for lines missing timestamps, or enrich with file modification time as surrogate
- **Large log sets and memory usage:** Replace awk with streaming aggregation using associative arrays flushed periodically, implement parallel processing with xargs -P, or consider sampling strategies for extremely high-volume traces
